course: dictionary
lesson: 2959
title: Drool: spit, wanting, and playing dead
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: To drool means to let spit run out of your mouth. Babies drool when their teeth are coming through, and dogs drool when they smell food. «A Virginia opossum chased by a predator may fall over, go stiff, drool and smell bad» tells you the opossum lets spit run from its mouth while it is terrified.
explain: Drool has a second, informal meaning. When you drool over something, you want it very much or admire it in a silly way. «Mia drooled over the puppies in the shop window» does not mean spit was running down her chin. It means she wanted those puppies very much.
explain: Drool can also be a noun, the spit itself. Context tells you which meaning is which. Ask: is someone doing something with their mouth, or is someone wanting something? Then check your meaning by putting it into the sentence.
fact: To drool means to let spit run out of your mouth.
fact: Drool (noun) is the spit that runs from someone's mouth.
fact: «Drool over» means to want something very much, or to admire it in a silly way.
fact: Saliva is the spit in your mouth, and it helps you digest food.
fact: Pavlov's dogs drooled at a bell, which is a famous science example of a dog reacting to something it has learned to expect.
exercise: «A Virginia opossum chased by a predator may fall over, go stiff, drool and smell bad.» What does drool mean in this sentence? | Sleep very deeply | *Let spit run out of its mouth | Hide its tail under a leaf | Run very fast to escape
exercise: Maya says, «I drool over that new bike.» What does she mean? | She has spilt her drink on it | *She wants the bike very much | Her mouth is wet from eating | She is going to ride it in the rain
exercise: Which word is a noun meaning the spit that runs from someone's mouth? | drooled | *drool | drooling | drools
test: Why do dogs drool when they see food? | Because they are cold | Because they are tired | Because their feet are wet | *Because their mouths fill with spit, which is saliva
test: Which sentence uses «drool over» correctly? | I drool over my homework every night | The rain drooled down the glass | *Mia drooled over the puppies in the shop window | I drooled over my spelling test
exam: Which word best replaces «drool over» in «Ben drools over the new games console»? | Hides from | *Longs for | Sings about | Breaks
